;;; `_` in a guard reads nothing, in silence — M1e2 **W24**, register row
;;; `W24/wild-in-guard` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1e2.2.24
;;; (2026-11-05) from the inventory of 2026-11-05's W-12 (its probe
;;; `wild_in_guard.ein`, the program unchanged), ruled by the user on
;;; 2026-11-05 (Q-M1e2.16 (1)).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact, `(p a)`, and three rules whose guard writes the wildcard `_`
;;; as an argument of `eq` or `neq`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A guard compares two values, and `_` names none: it stands for an
;;; argument of a pattern (`01_grammar.md`'s `WILDCARD` row). The user ruled
;;; that such a guard is refused at load, as a range and a head `_` are, with
;;; a line in Ein's spelling naming the rule and the clause. Every command
;;; that loads the program exits 1 (`ein test`: 2).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered, and why it does not any more ─────────────
;;;
;;; Until S1e2.2.24 the program loaded, and each guard compared `_` as a raw
;;; IR node, which is equal to no value and to another `_`: `(eq ?x _)` never
;;; held, `(neq ?x _)` always did, and `(eq _ _)` held. Root derived `(hit-neq
;;; a)` and `(hit-both a)` and no `hit-eq`, exit 0, with no word of the
;;; wildcard
;;; (`ein saturate --dump FILE` and `ein solve FILE` at `32736ac8`, release
;;; and debug; every mode and `ein oracle` the same). Since S1e2.2.24 the
;;; loader refuses it: *kb load error: rule 'e': its :match writes (eq ?x _),
;;; and _ names no value — a guard compares two values, and _ stands for an
;;; argument of a pattern*, and the same line for `n` and `b`.

(relation p Node) (relation hit-eq Node) (relation hit-neq Node) (relation hit-both Node)
(p a)
(rule e () :match (and (p ?x) (eq ?x _)) :assert (hit-eq ?x))
(rule n () :match (and (p ?x) (neq ?x _)) :assert (hit-neq ?x))
(rule b () :match (and (p ?x) (eq _ _)) :assert (hit-both ?x))
